    "lesson": "A decoder improves its guesses by passing confidence scores between bits and parity checks. Positive scores favor bit 0; negative scores favor bit 1. In this assignment, use a small chain so you can follow where each score goes: b0—c0—b1—c1—b2. Check c0 connects b0 and b1; check c1 connects b1 and b2. Each check requires its two bits to agree.\n\nOne round has two phases. First, both checks read the messages from the previous round and send fresh advice to their connected bits. A check's advice to one bit is based on the other bit, not on the recipient's own message. Then each bit adds its original radio score to the new advice it received. It sends a reply to each check, leaving out that check's advice. These are the same local calculations you met earlier; this assignment puts them together across the chain.\n\nHere is the chain for one round. The radio scores are [3, −1, −1], one per bit. At the start, each bit sends its radio score to its connected checks. Because each check here connects only two bits, its advice is simply the other bit's score. So c0 sends [−1, 3] to [b0, b1], while c1 sends [−1, −1] to [b1, b2].\n\nNow each bit combines its radio score with the check advice. The resulting overall scores for [b0, b1, b2] are [2, 1, −2]. To send a reply, a bit leaves out the check it is replying to. For example, b1 replies to c1 with 1 − (−1) = 2: its own radio score (−1) plus the advice from c0 (+3). That +2 is the new message c1 will use about b1 in the next round.\n\nThis explains why the order matters. Both checks must finish reading the old messages before any new bit-to-check messages are used. If c1 saw b1's new +2 during this same round, information from c0 would travel through b1 to c1 immediately, making the round depend on processing order. In a flooding round, all checks use the same old snapshot; all new messages are saved for the next round. Checks update whether or not their parity rule currently passes.\n\nThe starter uses dense arrays to make the graph easy to see: H, v2c and c2v have shape (2, 3), with rows for checks and columns for bits. A 1 in H marks a real connection. A 0 means there is no edge; message entries at those positions stay zero and are ignored. The channel and posterior arrays have shape (3,), one score per bit. Initialize v2c as H multiplied element-by-element by channel, so non-edge entries start at zero.\n\nFor the example, the fresh check messages are [[−1, 3, 0], [0, −1, −1]], the posterior scores are [2, 1, −2], and the new v2c messages are [[3, −2, 0], [0, 2, −1]]. Notice b2 still has score −2: evidence from b0 has not reached it yet. A second round lets c1 use b1's new +2 message.\n\n## Your task\nImplement flooding_step(h, channel, v2c) in python/iteration.py. Return (c2v, new_v2c, posterior), each with the shapes described above. Do not modify h, channel, or the old v2c array. This function performs exactly one round; it does not make hard bit decisions or repeat until the parity checks pass.",
